  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Harnessing the Demographic Dividend: Eu Sou Capaz</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Eu Sou Capaz (Safe Spaces) component of the wider GoM and World Bank Harnessing the Demographic Dividend programme aims to directly support the empowerment of out-of-school and vulnerable adolescent girls across 7 provinces and 50 districts in Mozambique.  as well as informal and formal education provision for OOSG. The project has two main components: 1) Safe Spaces targeting 200,000 OOSG aged 10-19 years through life-skills, mentoring and referrals to health, gender-based violence (GBV) services. At least 2 male community facilitators and 4 female mentors will be trained in each locality. This component also includes School of Partners, engaging 16,860 family members and intimate partners per year as agents of change to tackle GBV and advocate for SRHR and girls education. Additionally it includes wider community mobilisation and training of 30,000 community leaders to foster awareness of the programme and girls&apos; rights. 2) Keeping Girls in School component will provide 600,000 vulnerable girls in 3,260 schools with uniforms and bicycles to promote access to formal education. The full budget envelope is $11,700,000 to be paid in 12 instalments by the Client - the National Institute of Youth (INJ-SEJE). This is a payment by results programme based on agreed deliverables and targets. Plan Mozambique will subcontract two local partners - JOSSOAL and Action Aid.</narrative>
  </description>
